<Diet
  title="Diet chart for acne"
  description="What the evidence actually supports for acne and diet — glycaemic load, dairy — and the Indian foods wrongly blamed. By Dr Hitesh Aggarwal, dermatologist."
  slug="diet-acne"
  category="acne"
  reviewed="2026-10-17"
  concern={['Acne', '/conditions/acne/']}
  intro="Diet plays a smaller part in acne than most people are told, and a bigger part than dermatologists used to admit. Here is what the evidence actually supports."
>
  <div class="note">
    <h3>The honest summary</h3>
    <p>
      Two dietary factors have reasonable evidence behind them: a <strong>high glycaemic load
      diet</strong>, and possibly <strong>skimmed milk</strong>. Everything else commonly blamed —
      oily food, spicy food, chocolate, nuts, ghee — has little or none.
    </p>
    <p>
      Changing your diet will not clear moderate or severe acne on its own. It is a supporting
      measure alongside treatment, and it is worth doing because it costs nothing.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Glycaemic load — the one that matters most</h2>

  <p>
    Foods that spike blood sugar raise insulin, which raises androgen activity and oil production.
    This is the best-supported dietary link in acne, and it is also the easiest to act on in an
    Indian kitchen without eliminating anything.
  </p>

  <h3>Swap towards</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Whole wheat roti instead of maida-based bread, naan and bhatura</li>
    <li>Brown rice, hand-pounded rice, or smaller portions of white rice with more dal and sabzi</li>
    <li>Millets — bajra, jowar, ragi — a few times a week</li>
    <li>Whole dals and rajma, chana, lobia</li>
    <li>Fruit whole rather than as juice</li>
    <li>Nuts and roasted chana as snacks</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Reduce</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Sugary tea and coffee several times a day — this is often the largest single source</li>
    <li>Cold drinks, packaged juices, energy drinks</li>
    <li>Biscuits, rusks, bakery items, white bread</li>
    <li>Sweets and mithai as a daily habit rather than an occasional one</li>
    <li>Instant noodles and packaged snacks</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The practical version: <strong>keep the roti and rice, cut the sugar.</strong> Most people get
    most of the benefit from reducing sweetened drinks alone.
  </p>

  <h2>Dairy</h2>

  <p>
    The evidence points at skimmed and low-fat milk more than full-fat, and at milk more than curd
    or paneer. It is a modest association, not a strong one.
  </p>

  <p>
    A reasonable approach: if you drink a lot of milk and your acne is stubborn, try reducing it
    for eight weeks and see. Do not eliminate dairy entirely, particularly for teenagers or anyone
    whose protein and calcium intake depends on it. Curd and buttermilk are generally fine.
  </p>

  <h2>Foods wrongly blamed</h2>

  <table>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row" style="width:40%">Oily and fried food</th>
        <td>Eating oil does not put oil in your skin. Fried food is worth moderating for other reasons, but it is not causing your acne.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Spicy food</th>
        <td>No evidence of any link with acne. It may flush the face temporarily, which is different.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Chocolate</th>
        <td>The sugar in it matters; cocoa itself does not appear to.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Ghee and nuts</th>
        <td>No good evidence. Both are useful foods.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Non-vegetarian food</th>
        <td>No link. Eggs, chicken and fish are all fine.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Worth including</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Omega-3</strong> — walnuts, flaxseed, chia, fish such as rohi and surmai. Anti-inflammatory, with modest supporting evidence</li>
    <li><strong>Zinc</strong> — pumpkin seeds, sesame, chana, curd. Deficiency worsens acne; supplementing without deficiency does little</li>
    <li><strong>Vegetables and fruit</strong> — for fibre, which blunts the glycaemic load of the rest of the meal</li>
    <li><strong>Water</strong> — not a treatment, but dryness from acne medication is easier to manage when you are properly hydrated</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A day, put together</h2>

  <table>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row" style="width:26%">Breakfast</th>
        <td>Besan chilla or moong dal chilla with curd · or 2 eggs with whole wheat roti · or poha with peanuts. Unsweetened or lightly sweetened tea</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Mid-morning</th>
        <td>A whole fruit, or buttermilk. Not juice</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Lunch</th>
        <td>2 whole wheat roti · dal or rajma · a seasonal sabzi · curd · salad</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Evening</th>
        <td>Roasted chana, peanuts, or a handful of walnuts. Tea without sugar, or with less</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Dinner</th>
        <td>Roti or a smaller portion of rice · paneer, soya, chicken or fish · vegetables</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>What to be sceptical about</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>"Detox" juices and teas.</strong> No effect on acne, and often high in sugar</li>
    <li><strong>Complete dairy elimination.</strong> Rarely necessary, and a real nutritional loss for teenagers</li>
    <li><strong>Collagen supplements.</strong> No evidence for acne</li>
    <li><strong>Cutting out whole food groups.</strong> Restriction causes its own problems and rarely clears acne</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Give any dietary change eight to twelve weeks before judging it — acne cycles slowly, and
    shorter trials tell you nothing.
  </p>
</Diet>
